As progress in quantum computing accelerates, many organizations will soon need to migrate from classical public-key cryptography to post-quantum cryptography (PQC) to remain secure against future attacks. While security requirements are driving this transition, performance remains a practical concern: PQC schemes often use larger keys and more complex operations than today’s algorithms, and it is not yet clear how much overhead they introduce in real systems. For system designers and engineers, even modest increases in cryptographic latency can affect throughput, user-visible response times, and the scalability of deployed services.

The core problem this project addresses is the lack of simple, predictive models for PQC performance under realistic operating conditions. Existing benchmark studies report raw timing measurements for individual algorithms and implementations, but they typically present results as tables or plots tied to specific experimental setups. This makes it difficult for practitioners to answer questions such as: How much slower will algorithm A be than algorithm B for my typical message sizes? or What latency should I expect in the median case versus the worst case? In particular, there is limited work on modeling PQC latency as a function of algorithm type, operation type, and input characteristics, or on estimating not just average behavior but upper-percentile delays that drive capacity planning.

This paper tackles that problem by taking a deliberately simple, exploratory modeling approach to post-quantum cryptography latency under realistic operating conditions. Using timing measurements from representative PQC implementations and benchmark-style workloads, the study conducts a first-pass investigation of how algorithm choice, operation type, and basic input characteristics relate to end-to-end latency. Rather than aiming for highly optimized, production-ready performance models, the analysis uses straightforward regression-based techniques and basic data exploration to see how far naive models can go in explaining typical (median) and upper-percentile delays and in highlighting the most influential factors.

The objective of this paper is therefore to develop and evaluate simple, interpretable predictive models that estimate the latency of key post-quantum cryptographic operations across different algorithms and use cases. The resulting preliminary predictions and factor-importance insights are intended less as definitive performance guarantees and more as an accessible first step that can help practitioners planning PQC migrations anticipate performance trade-offs and make more informed choices about algorithm selection and system design.





